{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and leader of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born around 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli at a young age after his father's assassination in 1152. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi in 1164 during the Battle of Harim and was held prisoner for nearly a decade. *Imprisonment and Release* He was released in 1174 after a ransom was paid and he returned to Tripoli. During his imprisonment his cousin Bohemond III of Antioch acted as regent of Tripoli.\n\nUpon his release Raymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He was a strong supporter of the Ibelin family and their allies and was a key opponent of the rise of Guy of Lusignan. *Regency in Jerusalem* In 1174 Raymond III was appointed regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem on behalf of King Baldwin IV who was suffering from leprosy. Raymond III acted as regent until 1176 when Baldwin IV came of age.\n\nRaymond III's military campaigns were marked by a series of defeats and setbacks. He was defeated by Saladin at the Battle of Marj Ayyun in 1179 and again at the Battle of Hattin in 1187. The defeat at Hattin was a crushing blow to the Crusader states and led to the loss of Jerusalem.\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 shortly after the Battle of Hattin. He was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV who was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch. Raymond III's legacy is complex and multifaceted. He was a skilled politician and leader but his military campaigns were ultimately unsuccessful.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.011065074242651463}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.264974000354414e-06}, {"token": "